  "headline": "Inflationary reflation, not a growth scare: dollar, yields and copper all near highs",
  "body": [
    "The configuration reads as hot, inflationary reflation with a hard-dollar edge. The 10-year at 5.24% is up 27 basis points over the month and 108 on the year, near the top of its 52-week range; the dollar index sits at a 52-week high after a 3.3% monthly gain. Growth gauges rule out a scare: copper is up 19% year-to-date at the 91st percentile of its range and the copper/gold ratio has climbed 7% over the month. Gold, down 4.4% on the month and near the bottom of its range, is being priced off higher real yields and a firmer dollar, not bought as a hedge.",
    "The editor's note points to the Saudi-Yemeni escalation behind Brent's spike. The week bears it out as a modest safety bid: oil up 4.7%, gold up 1%, the 10-year down 7 basis points, bitcoin off 3%. But Brent is up under 1% over the month and 73% year-to-date, so markets are treating this as a resumption of an existing inflation pressure rather than a new shock, with oil volatility only slightly above its yearly median.",
    "The 30y–10y spread flattening 2 basis points to 36 is a bear flattening, consistent with rate and inflation repricing rather than a term-premium growth story. Bitcoin buying 12% more gold than a month ago, with calm volatility, says risk appetite survives 5%-plus yields. USD/CAD near its high despite $105 oil shows the dollar overriding commodity fundamentals; the dollar and rates, not geopolitics, carry the most weight."
